Let $v_r$ have children. By [F2], each child update is indexed inside $F_r$, so every extend-add is defined. By [F3], distinct child subtrees have no matrix coupling between their eliminated interiors: an edge between two such interiors would make the later endpoint a filled neighbor outside the earlier endpoint's parent path. Thus the combined child pivot block is block diagonal, $D=\operatorname{diag}(D_1,\ldots,D_q)$, and is coupled to the shared front by a block column $B=[B_1\ \cdots\ B_q]$ partitioned by child subtree. Each $D_h$ is nonsingular by the subtree induction hypothesis: its exact scalar factor has a unit lower-triangular factor and positive, hence nonzero, pivots. The resulting Schur update is $C-BD^{-1}B^*=C-\sum_hB_hD_h^{-1}B_h^*$, exactly the sum of the child contributions. Direct block multiplication verifies
$$
\begin{pmatrix}D&B^*\\B&C\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}I&0\\BD^{-1}&I\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}D&0\\0&C-BD^{-1}B^*\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}I&D^{-1}B^*\\0&I\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For this front's scalar pivot $D=[d]$, the trailing block is $C-bb^*/d$. Thus the subtree Schur invariant holds at $v_r$.
